WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.625
This slide calculates the same kind of metrics using
PostgreSQL FILTER syntax

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.650
The grain is still one row per subject_id The first
expression begins

00:00:10.662 --> 00:00:14.638
with COUNT star but FILTER tells PostgreSQL to count
only rows where

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:20.438
diagnosis equals Pneumonia The second expression
counts only rows where diagnosis equals

00:00:20.462 --> 00:00:25.562
Fracture Compared with the CASE version the
condition is visually separated from

00:00:25.588 --> 00:00:29.662
the aggregate Many people find that easier to read
when the goal

00:00:29.675 --> 00:00:33.988
is a simple filtered count sum or average Run the
query and

00:00:34.012 --> 00:00:38.425
compare the result with the CASE example For this
problem the numbers

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:43.625
should match because both statements express the
same analytical logic FILTER is

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.625
very useful when one result row needs several
related measures For example

00:00:48.788 --> 00:00:52.538
you could count completed and open admissions sum
online and in-store revenue

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.512
or average values for several categories in one
grouped query Remember that

00:00:57.538 --> 00:01:01.162
FILTER is a PostgreSQL feature and is not supported
in exactly the

00:01:01.175 --> 00:01:06.125
same way by every SQL engine The broader idea of
conditional aggregation

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.388
is portable even when the syntax changes
